I now have a complete picture of the owner-identity code. Here are my findings.

## Overview

This is a Spring/OfficeFloor PetClinic app. The owner-creation pipeline is wired declaratively in `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/officefloor/rest/api/owners.POST.yml`, which chains functions in order: `RequireOwnerFields → NormalizeOwnerTelephone → ValidateTelephoneLength → NormalizeOwnerEmail → NormalizeOwnerAddress → ValidatePostcode → RejectDuplicateIdentity → RejectCityAtCapacity → RejectDailyLimit → BuildOwner → AssignCustomerCode → AssignHousehold → AssignNamesakeCount → SaveOwner → AuditOwnerCreated → RespondWithOwnerCreated`.

Note: the acceptance test `AcceptanceTest.java` asserts a NEW target format `<REGION>-<HASH8>` that the current code does NOT yet produce — so there is an intended redesign here (see item 1 vs. item 7).

---

## 1. Where customerCode is built/generated

File: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/AssignCustomerCode.java`
Class `AssignCustomerCode`, method `public void service(@Val Owner owner, OwnerRepository ownerRepository)`.

Current format is `CITY3-LAST3-####` (3 letters of city, 3 of last name, 4-digit per-city sequence):
```java
String city = owner.getCity();
String city3 = city.substring(0, Math.min(3, city.length())).toUpperCase();
String lastName = owner.getLastName();
String last3 = lastName.substring(0, Math.min(3, lastName.length())).toUpperCase();
int sequence = (int) ownerRepository.findAll().stream()
    .filter(o -> city.equals(o.getCity())).count() + 1;
owner.setCustomerCode(String.format("%s-%s-%04d", city3, last3, sequence));
```
The `customerCode` field/getter/setter live in `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/Owner.java` (field at line 58, accessors at 112-118).

---

## 2. Membership number and check digit derived from customerCode

Both are derived in the MapStruct mapper `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/mapper/OwnerMapper.java`, on `OwnerDto toOwnerDto(Owner owner)`.

Membership number (line 23):
```java
@Mapping(target = "membershipNumber", expression = "java(owner.getCustomerCode() + \"-M\" + String.format(\"%02d\", owner.getRegistrationDate().getYear() % 100))")
```
So membershipNumber = `customerCode + "-M" + (2-digit registration year)`.

Check digit (line 28):
```java
@Mapping(target = "checkDigit", expression = "java(org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.function.owner.CheckDigit.of(owner.getCustomerCode()))")
```
The check-digit algorithm is a Luhn check digit over the digits in the customerCode (non-digits skipped), in `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/CheckDigit.java`, `public static int of(String code)`:
```java
int sum = 0;
boolean dbl = true;
for (int i = code.length() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    char c = code.charAt(i);
    if (c < '0' || c > '9') { continue; }
    int d = c - '0';
    if (dbl && (d *= 2) > 9) { d -= 9; }
    sum += d;
    dbl = !dbl;
}
return (10 - (sum % 10)) % 10;
```
(Also note `membershipLevel` at line 24 and `contactPreference` at line 26 are derived in the same mapper, though not requested.)

---

## 3. Where the audit record is built from customerCode

File: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/AuditOwnerCreated.java`
Class `AuditOwnerCreated`, method `public void service(@Val Owner owner)`. Emits to a dedicated SLF4J `"AUDIT"` logger:
```java
private static final Logger AUDIT = LoggerFactory.getLogger("AUDIT");

public void service(@Val Owner owner) {
    AUDIT.info("owner created id={} customerCode={} registrationDate={}",
        owner.getId(), owner.getCustomerCode(), owner.getRegistrationDate());
}
```
It is the `audit:` step in `owners.POST.yml` (line 68-71). The test-side capture helper is `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AuditLogCapture.java`.

---

## 4. Where locality is derived

File: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/mapper/OwnerMapper.java`, line 25 (in `toOwnerDto`). Note: locality is derived from POSTCODE (falling back to city), NOT from customerCode:
```java
@Mapping(target = "locality", expression = "java(owner.getPostcode() != null && owner.getPostcode().matches(\"20\\\\d{2}\") ? \"NSW\" : owner.getPostcode() != null && owner.getPostcode().matches(\"30\\\\d{2}\") ? \"VIC\" : owner.getPostcode() != null && owner.getPostcode().matches(\"40\\\\d{2}\") ? \"QLD\" : \"Sydney\".equals(owner.getCity()) ? \"NSW\" : \"Melbourne\".equals(owner.getCity()) ? \"VIC\" : \"Brisbane\".equals(owner.getCity()) ? \"QLD\" : \"UNKNOWN\")")
```
Rule: postcode `20xx`→NSW, `30xx`→VIC, `40xx`→QLD; else by city (Sydney→NSW, Melbourne→VIC, Brisbane→QLD); else `UNKNOWN`.

---

## 5. Where REGION code is derived from postcode

There is no standalone "region code" field in production code today — region is expressed two ways:

- Postcode range validation per city/region in `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/ValidatePostcode.java`, `public void service(@Val OwnerFieldsDto request)`: requires 4 digits, and for known cities Sydney 2000-2099, Melbourne 3000-3099, Brisbane 4000-4099:
```java
if ("Sydney".equals(city))        { valid = value >= 2000 && value <= 2099; }
else if ("Melbourne".equals(city)){ valid = value >= 3000 && value <= 3099; }
else if ("Brisbane".equals(city)) { valid = value >= 4000 && value <= 4099; }
```
- The postcode→region mapping itself (NSW/VIC/QLD) is the `locality` expression in `OwnerMapper.java` line 25 (item 4).

The canonical region ground-truth tables live in the test base `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceBase.java` (lines 227-256): `CITY_REGION`, `REGION_TIMEZONE`, `REGION_POSTCODES`, and helpers `region(city)`, `timezone(city)`, `validPostcode(city)`. The test doc comment at `AcceptanceTest.java` line 7 states the redesign intent: customerCode should become `<REGION>-<HASH8>` where REGION is derived from the owner's region.

---

## 6. Where normalizedTelephone / telephone normalization happens

Primary normalizer: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/NormalizeOwnerTelephone.java`, `public void service(@Val OwnerFieldsDto request)` — mutates the request telephone in place to E.164, rejecting with `MissingOwnerFieldsException` (400) if it can't:
```java
String e164 = E164.toE164(request.getTelephone());
if (e164 == null) { throw new MissingOwnerFieldsException(List.of("telephone")); }
request.setTelephone(e164);
```
The actual algorithm is in `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/E164.java`, `public static String toE164(String raw)`: strips non-digits, keeps a leading `+`/country code or assumes `+61` and drops a single leading `0`, returns `null` unless 8-15 digits.

The term "normalizedTelephone" appears as the conceptual input to the identity key in `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/IdentityKey.java` (`forOwner`/`forRequest`/`key`), which builds `E164.toE164(telephone) + "|" + lower(email) + "|" + householdId` and is itself mapped to `identityKey` in `OwnerMapper.java` line 27. Length validation is a separate `ValidateTelephoneLength` step in the pipeline.

---

## 7. Existing related tests

- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceTest.java` — the only active assertion: `coreCustomerCodeIsRegionHash()` expects `customerCode` to match `[A-Z0-9]+-[0-9A-F]{8}` (the `<REGION>-<HASH8>` redesign target). This currently would NOT pass against the existing `AssignCustomerCode` format (`CITY3-LAST3-####`).
- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceBase.java` — shared harness with the region/postcode/timezone/holiday ground-truth tables and the algorithm replicas tests assert against:
  - `region(city)`, `timezone(city)`, `validPostcode(city)`, `knownOwner(city)` (lines 244-265)
  - `sha256hex` / `shaHex(s, n)` — SHA-256 hex helpers for the `<HASH8>` (lines 282-299)
  - `luhn(String s)` — reference Luhn check digit, mirrors `CheckDigit.of` (lines 301-321)
  - `uniqueTelephone()` (line 102), membership-number/per-city/per-day comments (lines 37-39)
- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AuditLogCapture.java` — `AutoCloseable` that captures events from the `"AUDIT"` logger for audit-record assertions.

No dedicated unit tests exist for `CheckDigit`, `E164`, `AssignCustomerCode`, or `OwnerMapper` individually — coverage is only through the acceptance layer.

